  "evidence": "TEXT_G: Leave the EU to restore sovereignty; five‑year freeze and caps on immigration with loyalty undertakings; oppose official multiculturalism; large defence build‑up and NATO focus; ‘victims first,’ life‑means‑life, three‑strikes, tougher policing; workfare and benefit limits to long‑term residents; grammar/professional schools and discipline; flat tax and cutting quangos; support for monarchy/Church and civic ‘Britishness.’ TEXT_D: Pro‑EU and internationalist; humane immigration/asylum with earned citizenship and ending child detention; progressive tax rises on wealth/high earners; restorative justice and presumption against short sentences; civil‑liberty rollbacks of surveillance; strong equality/care measures (pupil premium, scrapping tuition fees, triple‑lock pensions).",
  "reasoning": "Because the profile weights Loyalty and Proportionality most (with moderate Authority), TEXT_G most directly appeals to national cohesion and merit/reciprocity—sovereignty, stricter immigration tied to loyalty and contribution, tough law‑and‑order, grammar schools, workfare, and a flatter tax. TEXT_D foregrounds care/equality and internationalism while soft‑peddling authority, so it maps less well to the electorate’s higher‑priority loyalty/proportionality orientation.",
